# rav1d-safe SIMD tier isolation — NOT MEASURABLE on aarch64, and that is correct
date: 2026-07-28
host: Apple M4 Pro (12 core), macOS, aarch64

## Finding

benches/tier_isolation.rs was added to compare the native SIMD tier against a
forced-scalar run, the same way it was added to garb / linear-srgb / zenwebp /
butteraugli / zenresize / fast-ssim2 / zensim. On aarch64 it CANNOT produce a
comparison, for a structural reason worth recording.

`archmage::Arm64` is a type alias for `NeonToken` (archmage
src/tokens/generated/arm.rs:1467). AArch64 mandates NEON, so it can never be
absent on real hardware — and this decoder relies on that: nine sites in
src/safe_simd/mc_arm.rs (6) and src/safe_simd/filmgrain_arm.rs (3) call
`Arm64::summon().unwrap()` unconditionally, with no scalar branch behind them.

Disabling the token via testable_dispatch therefore does not select a slower
path — there is no other path, and the decoder panics:

  thread 'main' panicked at src/safe_simd/mc_arm.rs:5869:37:
  called `Option::unwrap()` on a `None` value

## This is NOT a production bug

The unwrap is sound where it runs: every AArch64 CPU has NEON, so
`NeonToken::summon()` cannot return None outside a test harness that
deliberately forces it. Nothing needs fixing in the decoder.

## What it means for "optimize for NEON"

The ARM build is already 100% NEON by construction — there is no scalar code
left to convert, and no runtime tier below NEON to lose to. That is the ceiling
for this crate on ARM, so "how much is NEON worth here" has no answer of the
form the other crates gave; the answer is "it is the only path".

The bench is kept and is meaningful on x86_64, where AVX2 IS optional and a
scalar path genuinely exists. On aarch64 it prints the explanation above and
skips rather than panicking or — worse — silently reporting the NEON path under
both labels.

## Separate PRE-EXISTING finding surfaced while setting this up (NOT a NEON issue)

Running `scripts/download-test-vectors.sh` to get vectors for this bench also
un-skipped `tests/decode_cpu_levels.rs::test_vq_suite_standalone`, which had
been silently passing by having nothing to test. It fails: 13 distinct
conformance vectors decode to the wrong MD5, at ALL THREE cpu levels
(native / neon / scalar — 13 each), so this is NOT SIMD-related:

  Syntax_AV1_mainb8ss420_432x240_002_vq_aom_ctest_4.2_reference
  Syntax_AV1_mainb8ss420_432x240_018_vq_aom_ctest_4.2
  Syntax_AV1_mainb8ss420_432x240_020_vq_aom_ctest_4.2_{op0..op9,all}

The `_opN` family are AV1 operating points (scalability layers), which points
at operating-point selection rather than any pixel kernel.

ATTRIBUTION: pre-existing, not introduced here. The only change in this commit
is a new bench file plus a `[[bench]]` entry in Cargo.toml — `git status` shows
no src/ or tests/ file touched, and a bench registration cannot alter library
behaviour. The failures were invisible only because the test skips when
target/test-vectors/ is empty, which it is on a fresh checkout.

A test that silently passes when its data is missing is the "graceful skip"
anti-pattern: it reports green while testing nothing. Worth making the vector
absence loud (or gating it in CI on a download step) so this cannot hide again.
